    "marks": 5,
    "text": "If $\\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta = p$, prove that $\\dfrac{p^2-1}{p^2+1} = \\sin\\theta$.",
    "answer": "Proved",
    "solution": "$p^2-1=(\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta)^2-1=\\sec^2\\theta+\\tan^2\\theta+2\\sec\\theta\\tan\\theta-1$. Using $\\sec^2\\theta-1=\\tan^2\\theta$, this equals $2\\tan^2\\theta+2\\sec\\theta\\tan\\theta=2\\tan\\theta(\\tan\\theta+\\sec\\theta)=2p\\tan\\theta$. Also $p^2+1=\\sec^2\\theta+\\tan^2\\theta+2\\sec\\theta\\tan\\theta+1$. Using $\\tan^2\\theta+1=\\sec^2\\theta$, this equals $2\\sec^2\\theta+2\\sec\\theta\\tan\\theta=2\\sec\\theta(\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta)=2p\\sec\\theta$. Therefore $\\dfrac{p^2-1}{p^2+1}=\\dfrac{2p\\tan\\theta}{2p\\sec\\theta}=\\dfrac{\\tan\\theta}{\\sec\\theta}=\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta/\\cos\\theta}{1/\\cos\\theta}=\\sin\\theta$.",
    "marking_scheme": [
