    "text": "Prove that: $\\dfrac{\\tan\\theta}{1-\\cot\\theta} + \\dfrac{\\cot\\theta}{1-\\tan\\theta} = 1 + \\sec\\theta\\operatorname{cosec}\\theta$",
    "answer": "Proved",
    "solution": "LHS: $\\dfrac{\\tan\\theta}{1-\\cot\\theta}=\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta/\\cos\\theta}{(\\sin\\theta-\\cos\\theta)/\\sin\\theta}=\\dfrac{\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos\\theta(\\sin\\theta-\\cos\\theta)}$. Similarly $\\dfrac{\\cot\\theta}{1-\\tan\\theta}=\\dfrac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\sin\\theta(\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta)}=-\\dfrac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\sin\\theta(\\sin\\theta-\\cos\\theta)}$. Sum $=\\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta-\\cos\\theta}\\left[\\dfrac{\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}-\\dfrac{\\cos^2\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}\\right]=\\dfrac{\\sin^3\\theta-\\cos^3\\theta}{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta(\\sin\\theta-\\cos\\theta)}$. Using $a^3-b^3=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2)$, this is $\\dfrac{\\sin^2\\theta+\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta}{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}=\\dfrac{1+\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}+1=1+\\sec\\theta\\operatorname{cosec}\\theta=$ RHS.",
    "marking_scheme": [
     {
      "step": "Expressing $\\tan\\theta$ and $\\cot\\theta$ in terms of $\\sin\\theta$ and $\\cos\\theta$",
      "marks": 1
     },
